verdict.explanation:
The quoted sentence is no longer in the report. Line 172 of /home/user/AI-works/compression-generalization/search/README.md, as it stands in the working tree, now reads: "so the best of the families certified on a network is itself a valid bound, as long as at most 16 are certified (at most 12 were)." The committed version (1e46f9f0) still has "however many were run", but the current file already states the 16-family limit that the reviewer asks for.

The reviewer's count is also wrong. I ran analyze_search.merged_runs() myself (script at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-AI-works/72f9123c-7af5-51a5-9a6e-7922014be341/scratchpad/recheck/count_fams.py). It certifies these numbers of families per network:
- 12 on each of the 12 one-hidden-layer teacher4 and teacher8 networks;
- 9 on the one-hidden-layer junta and threshold networks;
- 4 on the two-hidden-layer maj5, parity3 and junta6 networks;
- 6 on the two-hidden-layer teacher4 network.

The maximum is 12, not 13. The extra one in the reviewer's count probably comes from restarts.jsonl, which does have two rows for teacher4 standard 512 seed 0 (k=4 and k=8). That also contradicts the reviewer's statement that no jsonl has duplicate runs. merged_runs() treats every restart run on a network as one family. It keeps the run with the lowest estimated bound, which uses only the training set and the selection pool, and it uses only that run's certification. The report says this in the same paragraph: "Within a family, candidates are compared only on the training set and the selection sample, and only the chosen one's certification is used." So "at most 12 were" is correct.
